  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThis Weekend...\n\nRidge will remain situated over the Central CONUS, with the \naforementioned boundary finally starting to move south. This will \ngenerally come through similar to a backdoor cold front, moving into \nour northeast and tracking southwest through much of northwest \nIllinois and eastern Iowa Saturday. This may leave the boundary \ndraped over our area midday Saturday, which can be a focal point for \ndiurnally-driven convection. There remains some uncertainty on \ncoverage, with timing largely confined to the afternoon hours. \nAlthough, any decaying convection from Friday night and Saturday \nmorning may also provide outflow boundaries for further shower/storm \ndevelopment. Overall forcing will remain weak, which should limit \ncoverage to isolated-scattered. Areas along/south of Interstate 80 \nwill continue to be of concern for very hot/humid conditions, where \nheat indices are expected to reach 100+ again. Thus, opted to extend \nmost locations currently under the Extreme Heat Warning through \nSaturday. Areas north of the boundary will experience a less humid \nairmass with air coming from the Great Lakes Region. \n\nSunday will largely be under the influence of the post frontal \nairmass, with the boundary stalled out from northwest-southeast over \nCentral Iowa. Being post-frontal, a much different airmass will be \nin place throughout. Temperatures will range from near 80 in \nnorthwest Illinois to upper 80s in southeast Iowa, with dewpoints \nlargely in the 60s to low 70s. These conditions will result in heat \nindices finally below 100 throughout, with areas south of Interstate \n80 seeing values in the low-mid 90s. While this is still \nunseasonably warm, the airmass will be more pleasant than we have \nseen over the last week for many.\n\nNext Week...\n\nRidge over the Central CONUS will start to track easterly, \nflattening as it does. Although, it will be slow to break down, with \nincreasing llvl south/southwest flow into the area resulting in\nincreasing heat/humidity again. There will be the chance for \nupper 80s to low 90s once again, with dewpoints increasing into \nthe low-mid 70s. Thus, heat index values will be on the rise as\nwell, increasing to near 100 again. Midweek and beyond, we will\ngenerally fall under weak zonal flow. Upper level pattern would\nfavor us falling under the path of passing shortwaves, favoring\na potentially active pattern. Although, this is still a ways \nout in the forecast, leading to plenty of uncertainty. NBM would\nfavor daily PoPs (20-40%) Tuesday onward, which could lead to \ntemperature forecast challenges as well.",
